In the early game at any rate, you'll find that *what* weapon you use
isn't as important as if your character is any good with it.

The key point is that Nethack implements a skills matrix, and in every
weapon class every character can have up to 4 ratings:  Unskilled,
basic, skilled, and expert proficiency in a weapon class.    You incur
penalties for using a weapon you're not familiar with, and gain
bonuses for using a weapon you know how to use.

So I would suggest looking at your skills with the #enhance command. 
This gives you a list of all your character's skills, and that is
dependent on your character class.  As you may have guessed, wizards
are skilled with different weapons than rogues are, who are yet again
different from knights.

So my suggestion would be to find out what your character was good at
and using those weapons, at least until you find a weapon that's
REALLY cool, then start learning how to use that.

Now a question -- do you want to be spoiled?  We can talk at length
and point you to huge spreadsheets describing what a weapon's to-hit
is, how much damage it does, the exact algorithm the game uses to
calculate hit and damage, and what not.  However, this spoils a lot of
people's enjoyment of the game, as for many half the fun is learning
this on their own.

So... do you want us to tell all, and possibly ruin your enjoyment of
the game?  Or are these bare hints enough?

